      "schoolId": "daoist",
      "essence": "Stored strength meets danger without forcing, allowing sincerity, nourishment, and right timing to gather effective movement.",
      "summary": "From the Daoist lens, Waiting is active conservation rather than delay: firm yang remains whole while danger stands ahead. Wang Bi emphasizes a centered, correct position that can hold strength without becoming trapped, so movement retains the possibility of effective action. Liu Yiming turns the configuration inward, describing true strength as something gathered and nourished gradually amid obstructing conditions. Both readings distinguish responsive timing from either impetuous advance or passive resignation. Their scopes differ—one foregrounds governing structure, the other alchemical restoration—but each treats accord with the moment as the condition that lets stored capacity cross difficulty.",
      "sourceTensionNote": "Wang Bi foregrounds position, centrality, and timely function; Liu Yiming recasts waiting as gradual internal-alchemical recovery. These are complementary scopes, not interchangeable doctrines.",
