      "schoolId": "psychological",
      "essence": "Waiting exposes the difference between anxious suspension and deliberate preparation grounded in clear perception and inner security.",
      "summary": "Psychologically, Waiting resembles a period when intention is active but external conditions do not yet permit direct movement. Uncertainty may narrow attention toward urgency, wishful thinking, or premature action, making delay feel like helplessness rather than information. The sources instead emphasize objective appraisal, nourishment, and the preservation of confidence while plans mature beyond immediate control. Relationally, this can appear as pressure to force reassurance or resolution before the situation has disclosed enough. Integration lies in holding purpose and receptivity together, so patience becomes an organized capacity for readiness rather than a denial of desire.",
      "sourceTensionNote": "Wilhelm emphasizes structural centrality and nourishment, Balkin contrasts calculated with apprehensive waiting, and Dening foregrounds ordinary emotional strain and self-care.",
